{"parentUuid":"f6ef82e6-f241-412f-a352-28b999e76917","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011CdzNQKpKfyGKZZfmHCq47","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The grant_url shifted to a YouTube search results page, which means another tab must have navigated there and triggered a new StartGrant that overwrote my previous oricon grant. Looking at the visible tabs, tab 3 shows a Google search, but somewhere off-screen a tab hit youtube.com/results and got the grant instead. Ah, I see it now — there's probably a stale blocked-page tab still open in Chrome from Matthieu's earlier usage, one that already has `unlock=1` baked into its URL from before my test started. When the watcher restarts and rescans the tabs, that leftover blocked page with the unlock parameter still embedded in it would trigger the unlock logic again, granting that old target URL and overwriting my fresh test grant. The unlock parameter in the URL itself doesn't get consumed or cleared, so it keeps re-triggering on every restart.\n\nBut wait, after the grant is set and the tab redirects to the target URL, it should leave the blocked page entirely — so it shouldn't stay parked on that unlock-enabled URL forever. Unless the target itself is a search results page rather than a free video, which means after redirecting there, the next evaluation would check if it's a free YouTube page (it's not, since results pages aren't feed/channel/video), then verify if the grant matches the canonical form of that results URL with the query parameters stripped out.\n\nThe canonical form for results pages strips the query entirely, so any search results page should match the granted scope regardless of what the actual search query is — which is probably intentional, though maybe overly broad since unlocking one search grants access to all searches. But that's not the immediate issue. The real question is why the grant_url got overwritten with \"results\" in the first place, which suggests a different tab must have triggered that change.\n\nI'm realizing there's likely a stale tab from Matthieu's earlier session still open in Chrome with an unlock parameter in the URL. Every polling cycle, the handler processes all tabs across all windows in order, and if this old tab exists, it keeps hitting the unlock branch and redirecting to that results page URL, overwriting my earlier grant. So the stale tab's grant matching is failing because it's comparing the canonical results URL against the oricon grant that's already active—they don't match, so it tries to start a new grant for the results page instead of recognizing it should be allowed.\n\nThis overwrites the single global grant slot with the results-scoped grant, which then causes my oricon tab to fail its next check since the grant URL has changed to results—it gets blocked again and redirected back to the blocked page. This is exactly the behavior we're seeing, which proves the single global grant slot is the core architectural problem.\n\nWith multiple tabs open simultaneously, each one fighting over that one slot, they're constantly revoking each other's access. This likely explains Matthieu's \"complètement bugué sur youtube\" experience—he probably has several tabs open (search results, videos, channels, subscriptions) and the periodic re-evaluation combined with the single slot causes constant flapping between granted and blocked states as different tabs steal the slot from each other every second. That's a much more severe issue and directly explains the constant bugginess.\n\nBut now I'm wondering whether that stale tab actually had unlock=1 in the URL before my test, or if it got auto-granted somehow. not yet clicked on the block page would only have the `u=...` parameter without `unlock=1`, and that parameter only gets added through manual JavaScript interaction. A background redirect to the block page never includes `unlock=1`, so if the tab somehow got re-blocked and bounced back to the block page, it wouldn't automatically trigger a new grant unless that `unlock=1` was already embedded in the URL from a previous manual click. So the `location.href=` call navigates to the unlock URL, then the AppleScript redirect changes the tab's actual address to the target, replacing the block page URL entirely. If the AppleScript redirect failed to fire (maybe a timing issue), the browser would stay stuck on that self-navigated block page with unlock=1 still in the address bar. Given Matthieu's screenshot showed exactly this frozen state on the oricon block page, it's plausible he has another tab in the same stuck condition with unlock=1 baked in.\n\nThat older tab with the search results URL and unlock=1 was likely left over from an earlier failed attempt before my fix. It never created a grant because the old buggy logic either prevented it when another grant was active, or it simply never got processed at the time of that click. Now that my code fix is in place and the watcher restarted to rescan all tabs, it's picking up this genuinely stale tab from Matthieu's earlier session.\n\nThe real problem is that with only one global grant slot available, multiple tabs tagged with unlock=1 are now competing for it. Each second when the watcher ticks through the tab enumeration, whichever unlock request gets processed last wins the slot and kicks the others back to blocked status. This creates the alternating flapping behavior where the grant keeps bouncing between tabs—a separate, deeper bug from the dead-end issue I already fixed, and it's clearly reproducible given what I'm seeing live.\n\nTo fix this properly, I need to move away from a single grant slot to a multi-slot system where each grant gets its own file stored in a grants subdirectory, keyed by the canonical target or channel ID. Then GrantMatches can check if any active non-expired grant file matches the current URL or channel, StartGrant creates or overwrites the appropriate grant file, and expired grants get cleaned up opportunistically during checks or periodic sweeps. The tricky part is adapting the Timer UI—since it currently watches a single grant_until file path, I need to figure out which grant's countdown to display when multiple grants are active simultaneously.\n\nThe solution is to have the Timer dynamically compute the relevant grant file on each refresh tick by scanning the grants directory and finding a grant whose scope matches the currently active Chrome tab's URL. The Timer already resolves the frontmost tab's URL every second via AppleScript, so I can extend that logic to match against the stored grants using either channel-ID matching (though Swift doesn't have built-in channel resolution) or simpler canonicalized URL path prefix matching. Alternatively, the shell side could maintain a convenience symlink or pointer to the \"current best\" grant for the Timer to read directly.\n\nBut this is getting complex. Given the effort budget and the principle of simplicity first, I should step back and find a cleaner design. A better approach: keep the single-slot mechanics (which matches the original design's stated intent of \"une seule autorisation à la fois\") but make it robust against stale tabs re-triggering conflicting grants. The key insight is that a tab sitting on the block page with `unlock=1` in its URL should only count as a genuine click once—after we've processed it and granted access.\n\nThe current logic checks if the grant matches or starts a new one, then always redirects. But if the redirect fails silently (stale tab index, window moved, timing issue), the tab stays on the same URL with `unlock=1` and gets reprocessed on the next tick, re-triggering StartGrant again. This endless fight loop only happens when multiple tabs are genuinely sitting on the block page with `unlock=1` simultaneously, competing for the single slot.\n\nThis unusual state comes from either leftover stale tabs from previous buggy sessions (which Matthieu likely had from clicking \"Ouvrir\" on multiple blocked videos over time, each getting stuck), or a user deliberately opening the unlock link in multiple tabs for different videos at once.\n\nWith my new code, all these parked `unlock=1` tabs now actively compete for the single slot, causing visible flapping where each blocked tab flickers between showing content and re-blocking every second — arguably worse than the old frozen state. The proper fix is to process only ONE unlock tab per iteration and ensure it doesn't get reprocessed after being redirected away, since the URL change naturally stops matching the block-page-prefix condition on subsequent ticks, assuming the redirect actually sticks.\n\nThe real problem emerges when redirecting tab A to its target immediately triggers the generic YouTube enforcement path on the same or next iteration: if another tab B with `unlock=1` steals the single slot (overwriting StartGrant) in the same iteration, then when the next scan happens, tab A's new URL no longer matches its own grant (which B overwrote), causing tab A to bounce back to the block page — this is the core multi-tab single-slot conflict.\n\nBut stepping back, the tool's stated purpose is single-user casual browsing where you focus on one thing at a time, which aligns with \"only one authorization available at a time\" being intentional design, not a bug. The actual problem isn't that multiple tabs can't unlock simultaneously (that's by design), but that stale tabs from old clicks keep perpetually re-triggering grant creation because their URLs still contain `unlock=1` and Chrome leaves those tabs open indefinitely.\n\nEvery time the watcher scans tabs, any tab still showing the block page with `unlock=1` re-fires the grant request, constantly seizing the slot away from whatever the user is actually trying to unlock right now. This creates a persistent phantom hijacker if a forgotten stale tab exists somewhere — which perfectly exp